g wrote:
> Does anyone know the historical reasons for having not just a
> TaskFailed event, but also bb.runqueue.runQueueTaskFailed and
> bb.runqueue.sceneQueueTaskFailed events?
>=C2=A0
> The reason I ask is because I am working on enhancing BitBake to
> print the logfile paths for tasks when it prints its final =E2=80=9CSumma=
ry:
> x task failed:=E2=80=9D thing at the end. (There is nothing more annoying=
 to
> me than having to scroll up past a gillion lines of C++ template
> error nonsense to find the logfile path. I usually end up just
> manually navigating to the temp dir in tmp/work/).
> =C2=A0
> The bb.runqueue.* events don=E2=80=99t carry much useful information, whi=
ch
> is unfortunate since they are the source that Knotty uses to generate
> the Summary message. The runqueue understandably seems to just have
> access to things like the task ID and base config variables like
> BB_SETSCENE_ENFORCE, etc. Whether or not a task was fakeroot is also
> propagated down. But I don=E2=80=99t think the runqueue is operating at t=
he
> right context or abstraction level to have easy access to the
> particular task details.
> =C2=A0
> Contrast this to TaskFailed, which comes from build.py. I think the
> na=C3=AFve approach would be to change Knotty to use TaskFailed for the
> Summary message, but then you lose the distinction between runqueue
> vs scenequeue task. Maybe it would be easy to just tag the TaskFailed
> event with that flag though? Or perhaps it=E2=80=99s already there - I
> haven=E2=80=99t looked hard to be honest.

These events come from different contexts. When a task fails, that
messages goes from the failing task and execution context to the
worker, then back to cooker, which then multiplexes the events back out
to all the potential UIs.

Runqueue runs in cooker context (not recipe context or worker context)
and reacts to the failures and other messages and then generates its
own events about what it thinks is going on.

The UI correctly responds to runqueue as being the definitive "what is
happening" events.

As you correctly guess, the reason the runqueue events don't carry much
information is due to them not being in the task execution contexts.
There is a event helper in lib/ui/uihelper.py and it collates
information from the task events which knotty then uses.

Yes, you probably could teach knotty to use that info directly and not
the runqueue events but there can be time lag between them and working
off runqueue always seemed like a better definitive approach.

The difference between setscene and real task context is important as
they are displayed (and should be displayed) differently in the UI.

